<!-- 11 -->
<section class="slide" data-title="Reproducibility">
  <div class="eyebrow">Reproducibility and engineering</div>
  <h2>Built to be checked, not trusted.</h2>
  <div class="grid6">
    <div class="card"><h3>CI on every push</h3><p>Lint, types, security scan, tests, an offline smoke run with provenance check, web build and Docker build.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Offline replay</h3><p>Every Cohere and Workers AI response used in the results is committed. <b>make judge</b> needs no keys and no GPU.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Provenance check</h3><p>A number in the README that is not in results/ fails CI. This deck and its Q&amp;A are generated the same way.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Agent-native</h3><p>CLI, FastAPI, an MCP server and a SKILL.md, so any MCP-capable agent can certify images.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Docker and Space</h3><p>One container serves the upload demo: PNG, JPEG or TIFF up to {{api.max_mb}} MB and {{api.max_mp}} MP, processed in memory, {{api.rate}} requests per minute per client.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>CPU only</h3><p>{{e9.inspect_p50}} ms per image (p50) on a CPU. No training, no fine-tuning, no GPU.</p></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="notes">Everything here is built to be re-run. Continuous integration runs on every push, every model response that backs a published number is committed and replayed offline, and a provenance check fails the build if a number in the README is not in the results files. The tools are exposed through a command line, an API, an MCP server and a skill file, and there is a Docker image and an upload demo with strict size limits. A full inspection takes {{e9.inspect_p50}} milliseconds on a CPU.</aside>
</section>

<!-- 12 -->
<section class="slide" data-title="Limitations">
  <div class="eyebrow">Honest limitations</div>
  <h2>What this does not show.</h2>
  <div class="cols c2" style="margin-top:34px;gap:64px">
    <ul class="pts" style="gap:16px">
      <li class="w">Cross-session shift: single-image absolute mode misses the false-alarm target on the upper fifth of unseen sessions.</li>
      <li class="w">Cause attribution is imperfect: blur and under-exposure are sometimes read as low contrast.</li>
      <li class="w">Illumination, debris and motion-blur faults are mostly missed at severity {{sev.mid}} and below.</li>
      <li class="w">The expert label is not an artifact label; the culture-state kNN uses expert-labelled neighbours.</li>
      <li class="w">Small, single-lab data: one public dataset, one microscope, {{n_sessions}} sessions.</li>
      <li class="w">Faults are injected by us; real-world fault prevalence is unknown.</li>
    </ul>
    <ul class="pts" style="gap:16px">
      <li class="w">Retrieval gold set was written from the corpus: optimistic.</li>
      <li class="w">Grounding checks are lexical; semantic entailment is future work.</li>
      <li class="w">Small samples: n={{n_base}} base images; n={{vlm.n}} per vision-LLM group; {{e5.n_cases}} grounding cases.</li>
      <li class="w">{{rc.n_unsupported}} of {{n_rules}} lab actions have no literature basis in the corpus.</li>
      <li class="w">Certificates are content hashes, not cryptographic signatures.</li>
      <li class="b">Research tool. No clinical use, no diagnostic claims.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <aside class="notes">Here is the list I would rather you hear from me. The shift problem is not fully solved in single-image mode, cause naming is imperfect, {{n_miss}} fault kinds are mostly missed, and the expert label is not an image-fault label. The data is one public dataset from one lab, the faults are ones I injected, several samples are small, and {{rc.n_unsupported}} of the {{n_rules}} actions have no literature support in our corpus. Certificates are hashes, not signatures, and this is a research tool, not a medical device.</aside>
</section>

