<section class="slide" data-title="Roadmap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Roadmap</div>
  <h2>What I would do next.</h2>
  <div class="road">
    <div class="card"><div class="tag">Sponsor-aligned</div><h3>Neural OoC and MEA modality</h3><p>The same certificate for electrophysiology and calcium-imaging traces, so data assets carry one standard of trust.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="tag">Data</div><h3>Longitudinal wells</h3><p>Score each well against its own history rather than one frame, which is where drift and culture state live.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="tag">Trust</div><h3>Entailment in the gate</h3><p>Replace lexical support with a semantic entailment check, the largest known weakness of the gate.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="tag">Evidence</div><h3>Larger corpus</h3><p>Re-run rule citations over a bigger corpus when the API quota resets, aimed at the {{rc.n_unsupported}} unsupported actions.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="tag">Validation</div><h3>Prospective study</h3><p>Certify a partner lab's frames as they are acquired and compare with their own rejection decisions.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="tag">Integration</div><h3>Instrument hook</h3><p>Run on acquisition so a failing frame is re-imaged while the plate is still on the stage.</p></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="notes">Next steps, in the order I would take them. The sponsor works on neural organ-on-a-chip data, so the first step is to extend the certificate to those modalities. Then longitudinal monitoring per well, an entailment model in the gate, a bigger corpus once the quota resets, and a prospective validation with a partner lab, which is the only thing that can show real-world benefit.</aside>
